;;; The alive-∅ record site BETWEEN LAYERS — the site `CO-M1` names, reached at
;;; stock config with no flags. **Fixed** at M1e S1e.3.1; this is the
;;; regression. Found and banked 2026-08-28 as M1e D1.
;;
;; `phase2` recomputed `alive` after a layer, found it empty, and recorded root
;; — which by then held this layer's singleton writeback `(not (p Z))` and the
;; kill cache's `(not (p A))` / `(not (p B))`, all written after root's last
;; saturation. `totality` matched that state and had not fired.
;;
;; Three enterings, one layer. The recorded state was
;; `(is-a A T) (is-a B T) (is-a Z T) (not (p A)) (not (p B)) (not (p Z))`; feed
;; it back as a program and this engine answered `Contradiction` on it.
;;
;; `record_node` now re-saturates a KB written since its last saturation and
;; declines to record one its own rules refute, so this file answers
;; `Contradiction` — the `-L` column, and the hand derivation: `{(p Z)}` dies,
;; so `(not (p Z))` is entailed; `mutex` then refutes every state holding any
;; `(p ?x)` and `totality` refutes the state holding none.
;;
;; **`-K` does not repair the old behaviour here** — the trigger is the
;; singleton writeback, not the cache — which is what made this the harder of
;; the two witnesses and why both were banked.
;;
;; ── 2026-11-03 — what it answers now, and what it is to answer ───────────
;;
;; The `Contradiction` above was the walk's, and its hand derivation read the
;; search's own negatives as the state's (Q-M1e.7 ruling A). Under R a node is
;; `sat(root ∪ C)` with nothing the search wrote, so no `(not (p Z))` is ever
;; stored, `mutex` and `totality` never fire, and R is 4 states, maximal
;; `{(p A), (p B)}` (M1e2 S1e2.1a.5's switch).
;; And `totality` concludes `(false)` from `(absent (p A))` over `p`, which the
;; hrule proposes: the shape the user ruled out on 2026-11-02 (*forbid*,
;; Q-M1e2.5), so the right answer is a refusal at load. Register row
;; W1/alive-empty-interlayer, re-opened at the revisit (S1e2.1.7).
;;
;; ── 2026-11-04 — refused ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;
;; M1e2 S1e2.2.5 built the refusal and the row is fixed: every question about
;; a commitment exits 1 naming `totality` over `p`; `ein saturate` still
;; saturates. The region claim this file carried stated R's four states,
;; which is what the program answered until then and the row keeps as its
;; alternative; a refused program has no R to check a claim against, so the
;; claim went. Deleting `totality` keeps those four states and is monotone
;; (M1e2 S1e2.4.1 § 2.1).

(relation is-a T T)
(relation p T)
(relation bad T)

(is-a A T) (is-a B T) (is-a Z T)

(hrule guess () :match (is-a ?x T) :assert (p ?x) :why "guess (p {?x})")

;; (p Z) dies in TWO steps, so the one-step pre-branch lookahead cannot see it
;; and it survives into the layer as an entering. Z sorts last, so its
;; entering is the layer's last and the two survivors are entered against a
;; root that has not grown yet.
(rule taint () :match (p Z)   :assert (bad Z))
(rule kill  () :match (bad Z) :assert (false))

;; Once Z is refuted, nothing may have p — ONE step from any (p ?x), which is
;; what empties `alive` on the recompute.
(rule mutex ()
  :match  (and (not (p Z)) (p ?x))
  :assert (false))

;; Totality as a saturation refutation: something must have p. Dormant at
;; phase 1 (no negative yet) and dormant in every fork entered before the
;; writeback landed — so the two live branches are genuinely alive.
(rule totality ()
  :match  (and (not (p Z)) (absent (p A)) (absent (p B)))
  :assert (false))

(query
  :goal   (p ?x))
